![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

To ensure robust fail-closed behavior and capture detailed diagnostics when reading upstream posture validators, we should wrap the path resolution, file existence check, and JSON parsing in a try-catch block, recording any errors directly as diagnostics.

```javascript
function loadUpstream(root, paths) {
  const diagnostics = [];
  const read = (label, relPath) => {
    try {
      const target = resolvePath(root, relPath);
      if (!target) {
        diagnostics.push(`path_resolution_failed:${label}`);
        return null;
      }
      if (!fs.existsSync(target)) {
        diagnostics.push(`file_missing:${label}`);
        return null;
      }
      return JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(target, 'utf8'));
    } catch (err) {
      diagnostics.push(`file_read_failed:${label}:${err.message}`);
      return null;
    }
  };
  return {
    rightsGateValidator: read('rightsGateValidator', paths.rightsGateValidatorIn),
    structuredPostureValidator: read('structuredPostureValidator', paths.structuredPostureValidatorIn),
    sourceImagePostureValidator: read('sourceImagePostureValidator', paths.sourceImagePostureValidatorIn),
    diagnostics,
  };
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When reading and parsing JSON input files, wrap the path resolution and file reading logic to catch errors and record diagnostics rather than throwing unhandled exceptions, ensuring the system fails closed gracefully.
</details>

